# cmd.exe reads a batch file by SEEKING to a byte offset, not by buffering
# lines. With LF-only endings its offsets drift, so it resumes mid-line and
# executes the tail of a comment as a command. The symptom is not "syntax
# error": it is a pile of
#
#   'ild' is not recognized as an internal or external command
#   'coord_main' is not recognized as an internal or external command
#
# — fragments of REM lines — followed by the script's own failure message,
# which is what you actually read and believe (2026-08-19: this cost an hour
# chasing a missing MinGW that was present the whole time; the probe for it
# worked perfectly when tested on its own line).
#
# So: batch files are CRLF in the working tree on every platform, checked in
# with CRLF, and never normalised. `* text=auto` is deliberately NOT set here
# — it would rewrite these on checkout and reintroduce exactly this.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell is not affected by the seek behaviour (it buffers), but it lives
# on the same machines and mixed endings in one directory invite the next
# person to "fix" one of them.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
